The '''United Capuyaquiran Republic''' ('''UCR''') is an island country in the [[Capuyaquiran Sea]]. It comprises approximately 41 islands with a combined area of about 497 square kilometres and an estimated population of 251,234. Its capital is [[Maruba City]]; its largest city is [[Capuya]], on the island of [[Warabi]]. The country is primarily [[Burawi]]-speaking and is a member of the [[Adonian Community]].

The republic's islands were historically inhabited by the [[Murani people|Murani]], [[Dukuri people|Dukuri]], [[Yakuri people|Yakuri]], [[Barani people|Barani]], [[Warabi people|Warabi]], [[Karubi people|Karubi]], and [[Waturi people|Waturi]] peoples of the eastern Capuyaquiran Sea. Many were tributaries of [[Guajicutea]] between the 1290s and 1360s and later came under [[Burawa|Burawi]] authority. The islands became the autonomous [[Capuyaquiran League]] under a Burawi protectorate in 1755, and the United Capuyaquiran Republic was established as an independent state in 1832 during Burawa's transition from an empire to a republic.

== Geography ==

The United Capuyaquiran Republic consists of roughly 41 islands spread across the eastern Capuyaquiran Sea. The largest island is [[Warabi]], which covers about 146 square kilometres and is the historic homeland of the [[Warabi people]]. Other significant islands include [[Maruba]], the historic seat of the [[Kingdom of Murani]] and location of the capital, [[Maruba City]], as well as islands of the Dukuri, Yakuri, Barani, Karubi, and Waturi peoples. The republic's total land area is approximately 497 square kilometres, giving an average population density of about 503 inhabitants per square kilometre.

== History ==

=== Pre-colonial period ===

The islands of the eastern Capuyaquiran Sea were settled by a number of related maritime peoples, including the Murani, Dukuri, Yakuri, Barani, Warabi, Karubi, and Waturi. From approximately 1290 to 1360, several of these islands became tributaries of Guajicutea: Maruba and Waturi through diplomacy and trade pressure, Dukira and Yakura through conquest, and Karuba and Bariba through trade pressure and conquest respectively. The islands remained connected to Guajicutean trade and its wider tributary network.

=== Burawi rule ===

Following the [[First Capuyaquiran War]] (1540–1543), the Murani, Dukuri, and Barani islands revolted against Guajicutea and were defeated by the Burawi–Guaji alliance. They were incorporated as the [[Badweyn Islands]] under Burawi authority. In 1564, the [[Kingdom of Murani]] was established as a Burawi protectorate covering Maruba, Dukira, and Waturi. During the [[Third Capuyaquiran War]] (1578–1581), Burawa annexed the remaining Greater Capuyaquiran islands. After the [[Burawi Imperial Split]] of 1709, the islands passed to the Kingdom of Burawa.

=== Capuyaquiran League ===

In 1755, Burawa's Capuyaquiran possessions were granted autonomy as the [[Capuyaquiran League]], an associated state under a Burawi protectorate. The League governed its own internal affairs while Burawa retained responsibility for defence and foreign relations. The League period preserved the islands' distinct maritime cultures while drawing them more closely into the Burawi language and institutional sphere.

=== Independence ===

In 1832, during Burawa's transition from an empire to a republic, the United Capuyaquiran Republic was established as an independent state. The new republic retained close ties with Burawa while developing its own federal institutions across the island groups. Maruba City, on the island of Maruba, became the capital.

== Politics ==

The United Capuyaquiran Republic is a federal republic. Its structure reflects the union of the former League islands, with the island groups retaining internal administration under a central government seated at Maruba City.

== Economy ==

The UCR has an estimated gross domestic product of approximately I$5.5 billion, equivalent to about I$21,750 per capita. The economy is centered on tourism and maritime services, supported by fishing, inter-island transport, and ties to Burawa and the wider Adonian community. The country has a [[Human Development Index]] of 0.762.

== Demographics ==

The UCR had an estimated population of 251,234 in 2025. The population is largely Burawi-speaking, reflecting centuries of Burawi administration and the League's integration into the Burawi sphere. The largest urban centre is [[Capuya]] on the island of Warabi; the capital, Maruba City, is smaller. Capuyaquiran society also preserves descent from the historic Murani, Dukuri, Yakuri, Barani, Warabi, Karubi, and Waturi peoples.

